## Why sim-calculated motion (Starblast’s real secret)

Starblast uses **hand-written 2D math** (velocity, friction, simple primitives) — not a full commercial physics stack as the motor — so client and server stay deterministic and cheap.

| Prefer | Avoid |
|--------|--------|
| Shared pure-math motor (`ShipPhysicsDriveLogic`) for thrust/turn | Divergent client vs server motor branches |
| Planar (XZ) flight with continuous curves | Twitchy snap facing / FPS locomotion |
| Same inputs → same result on both ends | Relying on PhysX alone for predicted motor feel |
| Presentation reads smoothed pose **once** | Extra LateUpdate lerps on top of NetCode |

**Titan Orbit motor contract (not “ban all Physics”):**
- **Drive** = shared Burst-safe math → writes `PhysicsVelocity` / yaw (`ShipPhysicsDriveLogic`).
- **Integrate + collide** = Unity Physics in `PhysicsSystemGroup` for ship↔world. Bounce/friction/asteroid ram use collision events (`ShipCollisionBounceSystem`).
- **Ship↔ship** = `ShipShipHullContactSystem` (two hull spheres). Do not also PhysX-pair ships — remotes are interpolated and will stack.
- **Wrap** = off (`ToroidalMapEcs.TopologyEnabled` is false). Do not add a wrap step.
- **Do not** add `Rigidbody` / PhysX GameObject forces, or a second NGO-style tick loop, for predicted flight.

```csharp
// ❌ BAD — camera/mesh follows raw sim (stepped / fights reconciliation)
followTarget.position = simLocalTransform.Position;

// ✅ GOOD — presentation after NetCode smoothing
followTarget.position = presentationPose.Position;
```

**Non-negotiable pipeline:**
`sim LocalTransform → NetCode prediction/interpolation → presentation pose → render/camera`
One smoothing owner. Do not double-smooth.

